@* A name on the sheet with what it means behind it, on hover and on focus.

   **The same idiom `RankRow` uses on an Ability, extracted so the sheet can use it too.** The
   trigger is the name itself and it is a real button: a `<span>` with a mouse handler is a tooltip
   only for people using a mouse, and this is the one surface where a reader most often does not
   know what the word means — Presence, Willpower, Danger Sense, "Plot Hook" are not
   self-explanatory, and a printed sheet has never said.

   **Never a `title` attribute**, for the reasons this app already records and has a test for: it
   never appears on a touch screen, is unreliable for keyboard users, cannot be styled, cannot be
   dismissed, and is announced inconsistently.

   **Supplementary and never the only place anything is said.** WCAG is explicit that information
   carried only by a tooltip is information some readers do not get, so the sheet still prints
   every name, every rank and every figure exactly as it did. This adds what a thing *is*.

   **The tip is `display: none` when shut, and the description is a separate `sr-only` copy.** A
   hidden element keeps its layout box, and an absolutely-positioned tip that keeps its box put
   real horizontal overflow into CI once already — while the `sr-only` copy is what
   `aria-describedby` names, so the sentence never leaves the document.

   **It draws the bare name when there is nothing to say.** A Power the rules data has no
   description for, or a sheet drawn without explanations, has to render exactly what it rendered
   before — otherwise the printed sheet gains a control. *@

@if (Text is { Length: > 0 } text && Enabled)
{
    <span class="term">
        <button type="button" class="term-name"
                aria-describedby="@Id"
                @onkeydown="Key"
                @onmouseleave="Restore"
                @onblur="Restore">@Name</button>
        <span id="@Id" class="sr-only">@text</span>
        <span class="row-tip @(_dismissed ? "dismissed" : "")" role="tooltip" aria-hidden="true">@text</span>
    </span>
}
else
{
    @Name
}

@code {
    /// <summary>The word as the sheet prints it. Printed either way.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Name { get; set; } = "";

    /// <summary>What it means. Null or empty draws the bare name.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Text { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Off draws the bare name too.
    ///
    /// <para>Here rather than at each call site because the sheet has upwards of forty of these
    /// and an <c>@if</c> around every one would be forty chances to get the condition wrong — and
    /// the one that mattered would be on the printed page.</para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Enabled { get; set; } = true;

    /// <summary>Whether Escape has closed it for now. Same as <c>RankRow</c> and <c>OptionRow</c>.</summary>
    private bool _dismissed;

    /// <summary>
    /// Escape dismisses it, which WCAG 1.4.13 requires and the obvious implementation leaves out:
    /// a tip that can only be closed by moving the pointer is not dismissable by somebody who is
    /// not using one. Moving off, or blurring, brings it back.
    /// </summary>
    private void Key(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        if (e.Key == "Escape") _dismissed = true;
    }

    private void Restore() => _dismissed = false;

    /// <summary>
    /// <b>Derived from the name rather than generated.</b> A fresh identifier per render is enough
    /// on its own to break the replay's strongest guard, which renders the same character twice and
    /// requires the two pages to be identical — the trap <c>Tooltip</c> and <c>RankRow</c> both hit.
    /// Two terms explaining the same word share an id, which is the one collision worth having:
    /// the description is the same sentence either way.
    /// </summary>
    private string Id => "term-" + new string(
        Name.ToLowerInvariant().Select(c => char.IsLetterOrDigit(c) ? c : '-').ToArray());
}
